@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON with four-space indentation by default, and is syntax highlighted unless you pass ``highlight=False``. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also pass a ``indent`` argument to change the number of spaces used for indentation.
+
+The JSON is rendered by the :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, which you can print or log directly with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('[false, true, null, "foo"]'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, so long lines are not wrapped. Under default printing, lines wider than the console are cropped to the console width. To keep full lines, enable soft wrapping by passing ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`.
+
+There is also a package level :func:`rich.print_json` function, which prints to the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line with ``python -m rich.json FILE``, where ``FILE`` is the path to a JSON file.
+
